(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-cms-exam-4-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 4 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 3-year-old boy is brought for a routine visit. His father had a heart attack at 40 and was found to have very high cholesterol. What is the most appropriate step regarding the boy's lipids?","choices":["Screen only after puberty","Start a statin without testing","Wait until age 9 to 11","Elective lipid screening now"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Elective screening is appropriate for patients as young as 2 years old with a family history of heart disease or high cholesterol. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man needs two agents. Which pairing is appropriate?","choices":["A beta blocker with verapamil","An angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor with an angiotensin receptor blocker","An angiotensin receptor blocker with a dihydropyridine","Two thiazide-type diuretics together"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The preferred combinations pair a renin-angiotensin blocker with either a dihydropyridine or a thiazide-type diuretic, because the classes act by complementary mechanisms. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman reports six months of breathlessness when she walks her dog and one episode of fainting while gardening. She has never had cardiac imaging. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Electrocardiogram alone","Transthoracic echocardiogram","Transesophageal echocardiogram","Left heart catheterization"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Any patient with symptoms suggestive of aortic stenosis (exertional chest pressure, shortness of breath or syncope) should get an echocardiogram. Transthoracic imaging shows thickened, calcified leaflets with reduced opening. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 18, 22"},{"q":"When should primary aldosteronism be screened for?","choices":["Every newly diagnosed hypertensive patient","Resistant pressure with unexplained hypokalemia","Only after three drugs have failed and sleep apnea is excluded","Only in patients with a known adrenal tumor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Each is a departure from the ordinary pattern, and together they define a group in which a single morning blood test can change the whole direction of management. An adrenal incidentaloma and early or familial hypertension are the other triggers. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration. Which test confirms the lesion?","choices":["Chest radiograph","Doppler echocardiogram","Heart catheterization","Electrocardiogram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid regurgitation is diagnosed with Doppler echocardiography, which shows the regurgitation and may show prolapsed, scarred or displaced leaflets. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has two days of breathlessness after a viral illness. His N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide is 90. How should the result be read?","choices":["Heart failure is likely","The result is indeterminate","Acute heart failure is ruled out","Acute heart failure is likely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An N-terminal fragment below 300 rules out acute heart failure with a negative predictive value above 90%, and 90 is also below the under-75 heart failure threshold of 125. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a fasting low-density lipoprotein of 215 mg/dL on repeat testing. Her thyroid function, kidney function and urinalysis are normal, and she has no atherosclerotic disease. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["High-intensity statin","Coronary artery calcium scoring","Moderate-intensity statin after risk calculation","Lifestyle alone for six months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low-density lipoprotein of 190 mg/dL or more is severe primary hypercholesterolemia: begin a high-intensity statin without calculating 10-year risk. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man describes episodes of pounding headache, drenching sweats, palpitations and pallor, each lasting about twenty minutes. Between episodes he feels well. His blood pressure is 176/104 during an episode. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Primary aldosteronism","Hyperthyroidism","Pheochromocytoma","Obstructive sleep apnea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The five Ps are pressure, perspiration, palpitations, pallor and tremor, and the paroxysmal pattern with well intervals is what distinguishes it from sustained hypertension. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has right-sided heart failure confirmed by echocardiogram. Her team now wants to know how hemodynamically severe it is. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Right heart catheterization","Chest radiograph series","Repeat echocardiogram","Troponin trend over a day"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Once right-sided failure is established, right heart catheterization measures its hemodynamic severity. The echocardiogram has already made the diagnosis. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with Parkinson disease reports lightheadedness and dimming vision within minutes of standing and fainted once in his kitchen. He is well hydrated. Supine he is 138/80 with a heart rate of 68; standing he is 106/70 with a heart rate of 70. What is the most likely cause of his syncope?","choices":["A vasovagal reflex on standing","Volume depletion from poor intake","A ventricular arrhythmia","Autonomic failure from Parkinson disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Parkinson disease is an example cause of neurogenic orthostatic hypotension, and a large pressure fall with almost no heart rate rise marks autonomic failure. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has palpitations and fatigue. There is a holosystolic blowing murmur at the apex that radiates to the axilla and is loudest in the left lateral decubitus position (lying on the left side). Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mitral regurgitation","Aortic stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation","Mitral valve prolapse"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blowing means regurgitation and holosystolic narrows it to the mitral or tricuspid valve; the apex, the radiation to the axilla and the left lateral position mark mitral regurgitation. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 48, 73"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man who received a heart transplant has exertional shortness of breath and fatigue but no chest pain. His daughter asks how he could have heart disease without pain. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["His shortness of breath rules out the heart","Transplanted hearts cannot become ischemic","Transplanted hearts can have silent ischemia","Chest pain appears only with a full infarct"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A transplanted heart is one of the settings for silent ischemia, along with diabetes and peripheral neuropathy: chest discomfort may be absent, and shortness of breath or fatigue may be the only signs. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 30"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension from volume loss is lightheaded on standing in the clinic but alert, with a standing pressure of 100/62. Which treatment is inappropriate in this setting?","choices":["Reviewing her medications","A norepinephrine infusion","Correcting the volume loss","Slow position changes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine, norepinephrine, dopamine, phenylephrine and vasopressin are listed for the emergency department and intensive care only. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with hypertension has never had cardiac symptoms. An echocardiogram shows left ventricular hypertrophy with preserved function. Which heart failure stage is he in?","choices":["Stage B","Stage A","Stage D","Stage C"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic structural heart disease, including left ventricular hypertrophy, is stage B, pre-heart failure. Stage A requires a structurally normal heart. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has breathlessness and orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat). His chest radiograph shows blunted costophrenic angles, cephalization (prominent upper-lobe vessels) and an enlarged cardiac silhouette. Which study best measures his ejection fraction?","choices":["Multigated acquisition scan","Right heart catheterization","Twelve-lead electrocardiogram","A repeat chest radiograph"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The radiograph supports heart failure with effusion, cephalization and an increased cardiothoracic ratio, but cannot measure function. The multigated acquisition scan is the most accurate for ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with a hypertensive emergency has her pressure reduced from 240/130 to 125/75 within thirty minutes. She then develops a new hemiparesis. What happened?","choices":["Hyperkalemia precipitated the event","She developed rebound hypertension","The reduction was appropriate and the deficit is unrelated","Autoregulation had adapted to the higher pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her organs had reset the range over which they protect their own perfusion, so a value that would be safe in anyone else left her brain underperfused. Reducing it that fast left her brain underperfused. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with pulmonary hypertension has pulmonic regurgitation. She has no symptoms of right heart failure. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Treat the underlying condition","Pulmonic balloon valvuloplasty","Transcatheter valve replacement","Lifelong warfarin therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management of pulmonic regurgitation is to treat the underlying condition -- here the pulmonary hypertension, the high-pressure cause -- with referral when it is seen on echo or right heart failure develops. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 64, 61"},{"q":"In the natural history staging of heart failure, what is stage D called?","choices":["Symptomatic heart failure","Advanced heart failure","Pre-heart failure","At risk for heart failure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stage D is advanced heart failure, alongside stage A at risk, stage B pre-heart failure and stage C symptomatic heart failure. Stages C and D share the treatment columns sorted by ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 30%) on sacubitril-valsartan, a beta blocker, spironolactone and empagliflozin develops atrial fibrillation, and rate control is being planned. Which medication should be avoided?","choices":["Spironolactone","Empagliflozin","Diltiazem","Sacubitril-valsartan"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diltiazem is a calcium channel blocker, and calcium channel blockers are avoided in heart failure. His existing beta blocker belongs to core therapy. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Why does left ventricular involvement matter in coronary occlusion?","choices":["It rarely leads to heart failure","It spares the conduction system","It causes only silent ischemia","It carries higher mortality"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The left ventricle is supplied by the anterior interventricular, circumflex, left marginal and diagonal arteries, and left ventricular involvement carries a higher mortality rate. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How many criteria establish metabolic syndrome?","choices":["Any two of five","Any three of five","All five together","Any four of six"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Any 3 of abdominal obesity by waist circumference, triglyceride above 150, low high-density lipoprotein, blood pressure above 130/85 and fasting glucose above 110 establish it. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a week of fatigue and mild breathlessness. Her B-type natriuretic peptide is 250. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Acute heart failure is likely","Indeterminate for heart failure","Heart failure is likely","Acute heart failure is ruled out"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Between 100 and 400 the peptide has no useful sensitivity or specificity for heart failure, so the clinical picture and imaging have to settle the question. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Nitroglycerin is withheld in a right ventricular infarct because the injured right ventricle depends on what?","choices":["A slow heart rate","Extra oxygen","Relief of spasm","Adequate preload"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The infarcted right ventricle is preload dependent: nitroglycerin decreases preload, which reduces the Frank-Starling effect on its myocardium, so it fails into right-sided heart failure, output drops and shock follows. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man remains above goal despite the described sequence: adherence confirmed, diuretic optimized, three agents titrated, spironolactone added and tolerated. What now?","choices":["Add a second mineralocorticoid antagonist","Refer to a hypertension specialist","Accept the current pressure as his individual goal","Stop spironolactone and restart from a single agent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Referral is reserved for truly difficult cases after the full sequence has been worked through rather than being an early substitute for it. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with a mechanical mitral valve asks whether she could take daily aspirin instead of her current anticoagulant. Which long-term therapy is recommended for her?","choices":["Aspirin in place of it","Heparin every day","A vitamin K antagonist","No drug after one year"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With a mechanical heart valve, vitamin K antagonist therapy is recommended over no vitamin K antagonist and over antiplatelet agents. Warfarin is the one most commonly used. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man with six hours of atypical chest discomfort has a normal electrocardiogram and two high-sensitivity troponin I values of 4 ng/L. Which disposition is most appropriate?","choices":["Start thrombolytic therapy","Admit to intensive care","Discharge with follow-up","Urgent cardiac catheterization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Troponin is a tool that says who can safely go home. Two high-sensitivity troponin I values of 4 ng/L, far below the men's 20 ng/L, with a normal electrocardiogram rule out an infarct. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"Which two broad mechanisms lower pressure enough to cause cerebral hypoperfusion?","choices":["Raised brain pressure or a seizure focus","Low glucose or low oxygen delivery","Low peripheral resistance or low output","High peripheral resistance or high output"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Reflex and autonomic causes lower peripheral resistance, while volume depletion, venous pooling and cardiac causes lower cardiac output; either leaves the brain underperfused. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman has low-density lipoprotein of 290 mg/dL with a normal triglyceride. She has firm nodules along both Achilles tendons (tendon xanthomas), and her father had a heart attack at 48. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mixed dyslipidemia","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Severe hypertriglyceridemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Low-density lipoprotein of 200 to 400 mg/dL, normal triglyceride, Achilles tendon xanthomas (present in about 75%) and premature coronary disease in a first-degree relative fit one mutant allele. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman starting home monitoring calls alarmed because one reading was 168/102, although her seven-day average is 128/80. What should she be told?","choices":["Reassure her that the average matters, not one reading","Increase her medication on the basis of the highest reading","Tell her home readings are unreliable and to stop monitoring","Send her to the emergency department"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Overreacting to an outlier generates anxiety and unnecessary changes in therapy, which is why the schedule is built around averaging fourteen readings. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man is admitted with a large myocardial infarction and develops hypotension, cool extremities and falling urine output from cardiogenic shock. Which infarct feature makes this complication more common?","choices":["A small subendocardial infarct","A right-sided infarct","Reperfusion inside 90 minutes","A left-sided infarct"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cardiogenic shock is more common in left-sided myocardial infarctions, and left ventricular involvement carries a higher mortality. The left ventricle supplies the systemic output that shock takes away. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 78"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with a family history of heart failure smokes, drinks heavily and is obese. Her heart is structurally normal. Which counseling point best lowers her heart failure risk?","choices":["Start a loop diuretic as prevention","Family history cannot be offset at all","Wait for symptoms before changing","Quit tobacco, cut alcohol, lose weight"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tobacco, excessive alcohol and obesity are modifiable risk factors for heart failure. Her family history is a risk factor she cannot change, so counseling targets the ones she can. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the treatment for a patient with familial hypercholesterolemia refractory to drug therapy?","choices":["A very low-fat diet alone","Evolocumab or alirocumab","Low-intensity statin instead","Switch the statin to a fibrate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients refractory to drug therapy may be treated with a proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin type 9 inhibitor such as evolocumab or alirocumab, after high-intensity statins with ezetimibe and nicotinic acid. It is not a first step. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman who smokes has recurrent chest pain at rest between 3 and 5 a.m., each episode lasting about 10 minutes. An electrocardiogram during one episode shows transient ST-segment elevation that resolves, and coronary angiography shows no significant stenosis. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Unstable angina","Stable angina","Vasospastic angina"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vasospastic (Prinzmetal) angina occurs at rest, most often between midnight and early morning, in younger patients who smoke, with transient ST-segment elevation during an episode and no high-grade stenosis. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 88, 89, 90"},{"q":"A 43-year-old man has a fasting triglyceride of 620 mg/dL. He drinks heavily most nights. Before any drug is started, which additional conditions should be evaluated?","choices":["Diabetes, kidney disease, hypothyroidism","Lipoprotein(a) and coronary calcium","Tendon xanthomas and corneal arcus","Anorexia nervosa and biliary cirrhosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With triglyceride of 500 or more rule out diabetes mellitus, chronic kidney disease, alcoholism, pregnancy and hypothyroidism; his alcohol history is important too. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"What characterizes primary, or essential, hypertension?","choices":["Blood pressure that responds to no medication","No single identifiable underlying cause","Onset before the age of thirty in most patients","An identifiable underlying condition in most cases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is a diagnosis of pattern rather than of mechanism, which is why the clinical work is about recognizing when the pattern does NOT fit and a secondary cause should be sought. It accounts for the vast majority of adult cases. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man arrives at the emergency department with 30 minutes of chest pressure. Which pair of studies does every patient with chest pain receive?","choices":["Calcium score and a lipid panel","Electrocardiogram and chest radiograph","Treadmill stress test and D-dimer","Coronary angiography and echocardiogram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All chest pain patients get a chest radiograph and a stat electrocardiogram. Blood work (complete blood count, metabolic panel, coagulation studies, troponin) follows. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has had diarrhea and poor oral intake for 3 days and fainted when he stood up quickly this morning. His oral mucous membranes are dry. Supine he is 134/76 with a heart rate of 66; standing he is 104/62 with a heart rate of 94. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Postural orthostatic tachycardia","Situational reflex syncope"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A 30 mmHg systolic fall with a heart rate rise from 66 to 94 shows an intact sympathetic reflex, and diarrhea with poor intake explains the volume depletion. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with a known aortic dissection develops sudden aortic regurgitation with cardiogenic shock and pulmonary edema. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Repeat echocardiogram in six months","Emergency valve replacement or repair","Percutaneous balloon valve dilation","Outpatient afterload reduction therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute severe aortic regurgitation is treated with emergency aortic valve replacement or repair, ideally within 24 hours. Acute causes include myocardial infarction, endocarditis, aortic dissection and trauma. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 31, 26"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman without diabetes has low-density lipoprotein of 120 mg/dL and a 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 3%. She has no risk-enhancing factors. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Ezetimibe alone","Moderate-intensity statin","High-intensity statin","Emphasize lifestyle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low risk (under 5%) calls for a risk discussion that emphasizes lifestyle to reduce risk factors, rather than a statin. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension has leg edema, crackles and breathlessness climbing stairs. Echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 62% and B-type natriuretic peptide is 640. Which therapy carries the strongest recommendation now?","choices":["An inotrope such as dobutamine","Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate","A diuretic as needed for congestion","A beta blocker titrated to heart rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With preserved ejection fraction and congestion, diuretics as needed carry the strong recommendation. A sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor is next at moderate strength; the neprilysin combination, antagonist and receptor blocker are weak. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia and under 2% of normal receptor activity asks, through his parents, why he needs frequent sessions attached to a machine. Which explanation is most appropriate?","choices":["It replaces his missing liver cells","It filters the bad cholesterol out","It cures the disorder in weeks","It lowers his triglyceride level"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Apheresis is like dialysis: the elective removal of low-density particles from the circulation, and it is the treatment of choice for the homozygous disorder. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 61-year-old with abrupt severe interscapular pain has symmetric, normal-volume pulses in all four limbs. What follows?","choices":["An aortic regurgitation murmur would be required to proceed","Dissection is excluded and another cause should be sought","A normal chest radiograph would then exclude it","Normal pulses do not exclude dissection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Absent pulse findings specifically do not exclude the diagnosis, which is why a normal pulse examination must not close the question. Imaging is still driven by the clinical suspicion. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with hypertriglyceridemia has a total cholesterol of 250 mg/dL, high-density lipoprotein of 40 mg/dL and triglyceride of 300 mg/dL. What is his non-high-density cholesterol?","choices":["190 mg/dL","210 mg/dL","290 mg/dL","50 mg/dL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Non-high-density cholesterol is total cholesterol minus high-density cholesterol: 250 minus 40 is 210 mg/dL. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man is recovering from acute heart failure after an infarction with an ejection fraction of 29%. He wears a defibrillator vest while his guideline-directed therapy is being optimized. Which referral should accompany this phase?","choices":["Valve surgery review","Transplant evaluation","Cardiac rehabilitation","Hospice enrollment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After acute heart failure with an ejection fraction below 35%, the pathway pairs optimized guideline-directed therapy with cardiac rehabilitation while he wears the vest, before the echocardiogram is repeated. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has chest pain with ST elevation in V1 through V4. Which pattern of ischemia does ST elevation reflect?","choices":["Transmural ischemia","Microvascular dysfunction","Reversible demand ischemia","Subendocardial ischemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Transmural ischemia, involving the full thickness of the wall, produces ST elevation in the leads over that region. Subendocardial ischemia of the inner wall produces ST depression instead. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which description fits the tricuspid stenosis murmur?","choices":["Holosystolic murmur near the epigastrium, louder on inspiration","Diastolic rumble at the apex, best on the left side","Harsh mid-systolic murmur at the left upper sternal border","Mid-diastolic rumble near the xiphoid, louder on inspiration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid stenosis gives a soft opening snap and a mid-diastolic rumble at the left lower sternal border near the xiphoid process, increasing with inspiration, with jugular vein distension. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man reports lightheadedness and dimming vision within a minute or two of standing, which settles when he sits. His examination at rest is normal. Which test should be performed first?","choices":["Orthostatic vital signs","A tilt-table test","An echocardiogram","A stress test"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pressure and heart rate taken supine and again within 3 minutes of standing show whether orthostatic criteria are met and which type the heart rate response suggests. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 49-year-old newly diagnosed hypertensive patient has fundoscopy showing generalized arteriolar narrowing and arteriovenous nicking, with no hemorrhages or exudates. What does this tell you?","choices":["They reflect current and previous pressure","These changes reflect current blood pressure only","These findings carry no prognostic weight","These findings indicate a hypertensive emergency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Generalized narrowing and nicking record the accumulated burden, whereas focal narrowing, hemorrhages and exudates relate to CURRENT pressure only. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with a myocardial infarction three years ago has breathlessness on exertion and orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat). Blood pressure is 104/68. There is a third heart sound (an extra sound in early diastole just after the second heart sound) and bibasilar crackles. B-type natriuretic peptide is 820. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Diastolic failure from hypertrophic disease","Isolated right-sided failure from the lungs","Systolic failure from ischemic cardiomyopathy","High-output failure from increased demand"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Every link of the systolic chain is here: ischemic cardiomyopathy after infarction, a low-normal blood pressure, a third heart sound from rapid filling of a dilated ventricle, and pulmonary edema, confirmed by an elevated natriuretic peptide. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman has grade II diastolic dysfunction with an ejection fraction of 46%. She has fatigue and breathlessness with exertion but has never had pulmonary or peripheral edema and has never needed a diuretic. Does she meet a clinical diagnosis of congestive heart failure?","choices":["No, because congestion is required","Yes, because she has symptoms","Yes, because she is older than 65","Yes, because her fraction is 46%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A clinical diagnosis of congestive heart failure requires congestion, meaning pulmonary or peripheral edema. She has symptoms and a mildly reduced ejection fraction, but without edema the congestive label does not apply. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What heart rate rise defines postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome in adolescents?","choices":["An increase of 20 beats per minute","An increase of 30 beats per minute","An increase of 10 beats per minute","An increase of 40 beats per minute"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In adolescents the threshold is an increase of 40 beats per minute, higher than the adult rise of more than 30, and adolescence is when the syndrome is often first considered. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with rheumatic mitral stenosis has an echocardiogram showing a markedly enlarged left atrium. Which complication is she at greatest risk of developing?","choices":["Left ventricular dilation","Concentric hypertrophy","Aortic root dilation","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In mitral stenosis the left atrium enlarges under raised pressure, and left atrial enlargement predisposes to an abnormal rhythm such as atrial fibrillation, which is common in severe disease. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 35, 39"},{"q":"What treatment does mild to moderate pulmonic stenosis need?","choices":["None","A loop diuretic","Balloon valvuloplasty","Valve replacement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mild to moderate pulmonic stenosis needs no treatment and carries a 94% 20-year survival. Severe symptomatic disease is treated with diuretics and pulmonic balloon valvuloplasty. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 66-year-old African American man with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 25%) remains short of breath with slight exertion despite optimized sacubitril-valsartan, a beta blocker, spironolactone and dapagliflozin. Which addition is recommended?","choices":["Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate","Dobutamine continued at home","Verapamil for additional rate control","Isosorbide mononitrate on its own"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For African American patients with reduced ejection fraction in functional class III or IV, hydralazine with nitrates carries a strong recommendation on top of the core classes. Slight-exertion symptoms put him in functional class III. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man is seen in the emergency department after fainting while voiding at night. His examination is normal. Which test excludes hypoglycemia as the cause?","choices":["Orthostatic vital signs","A tilt-table test","A blood glucose level","An echocardiogram"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A normal blood glucose rules out hypoglycemia, a non-hypotensive cause, as blood counts and a metabolic panel address infection and metabolic causes. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman standing at an outdoor event in the heat develops tunnel vision, lightheadedness and dizziness but is still conscious. If her pressure keeps falling, what is likely to follow?","choices":["A generalized seizure","Supine hypertension","A prolonged coma","A brief faint"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her symptoms are near-syncope, which precedes syncope: a transient, self-limited loss of consciousness followed by spontaneous recovery. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man's pressure is 100/64, which is normal for many adults but far below his usual 150/90, and he now feels lightheaded. How should this reading be interpreted?","choices":["As proof of autonomic failure","As a measurement artifact","As relative hypotension for him","As normal, since it is above 90"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypotension is a fall below normal, and normal is judged against the patient's age, comorbidities and own baseline, so a relative fall can be significant. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man is diagnosed with a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction. His oxygen saturation (SpO2) is 97% on room air and his heart rate is 88. Which is part of his initial treatment?","choices":["Supplemental oxygen","A calcium channel blocker","Chewable aspirin","A thrombolytic agent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Unstable angina and non-ST-elevation infarction are a medical emergency treated alike: chewable aspirin, a beta blocker and intravenous nitrates if appropriate, and dual antiplatelet therapy with or without anticoagulation. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 49, 80"},{"q":"Which drug class is recommended to prevent heart failure in patients with diabetes who have no structural heart disease?","choices":["Angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor","Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate","Beta blocker started at diagnosis","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prevention in stage A, risk factors with a normal heart, carries one strong drug recommendation: a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor for patients with diabetes, ahead of any beta blocker, which is a stage B therapy. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with fatigue, cold intolerance and constipation is found to have blood pressure of 146/96 with a predominantly diastolic elevation. Which mechanism fits?","choices":["Autonomous aldosterone secretion","Hypothyroidism raising vascular resistance","Hyperthyroidism raising cardiac output","Catecholamine excess from an adrenal tumor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypothyroidism raises systemic vascular resistance, which lifts the diastolic pressure, whereas hyperthyroidism raises cardiac output and lifts the systolic. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"}]};
